Newspeak3
'Root'
class JSForCroquet usingPlatform: platform js: jsForPsoup = Object new (
	|
		private JsForPsoup <JSForPrimordialSoup class> = jsForPsoup.
	|
) (
	public class Js usingPlatform: p = JsForPsoup usingPlatform: p (
		|
			sessionLocalStorage <SessionStorage>
		|
	) (
		class SessionStorage new = Object new (
			(* A localStorage-shaped store private to THIS Croquet instance.

	   It is deliberately in memory rather than in the browser's localStorage.
	   The Croquet store has always been wiped on first touch each startup - all
	   clients must begin from the same state, and the only state they can agree
	   on locally is none - so nothing it holds ever outlives the page, and
	   nothing is lost by not writing it to disk. What IS gained is isolation:
	   several instances of a session can run in one browser, and a browser
	   store keyed by session id would have them share one bucket, where the
	   next one to start wiped the live one's data. An object per instance
	   cannot collide with anything, and leaves no keys behind.

	   Values live on a plain JS object rather than a Newspeak Map because this
	   class sits in the js module, below collections. Absence is probed with
	   hasOwnProperty - a bare read of a missing key on a plain object can
	   answer Object.prototype's doesNotUnderstand catcher rather than nil.

	   `global` reaches the enclosing Js only because Js declares a forwarding
	   override of it - see there. *)
			
			|
				private JSObject <Alien> = global at: #Object.
				entries <Alien[JSObject]> ::= JSObject new.
			|
		) (
			public clear = (
				entries:: JSObject new
			)
			public getItem: key <String> ^<String | UndefinedObject> = (
				(entries hasOwnProperty: key) ifFalse: [ ^nil ].
				^entries at: key
			)
			public removeItem: key <String> = (
				(entries hasOwnProperty: key) ifTrue: [ entries at: key put: nil ]
			)
			public setItem: key <String> to: value = (
				(* Stores the STRING form, as localStorage does and as the JS shim this
		   replaces did ("match localStorage behavior"). Callers rely on it:
		   HopscotchWebIDE writes timestamps as Integers and reads them back
		   through `Integer parse:radix:`, which wants a string - keeping the
		   Integer made that answer doesNotUnderstand on #size.

		   printString only for non-Strings: a String's printString is quoted,
		   which would corrupt every ordinary value. *)
				
				entries at: key
					put: (value isKindOfString ifTrue: [ value ] ifFalse: [ value printString ])
			)
		) : ()
		public global ^<Alien> = (
			(* Forwards to the inherited slot, and exists only to bring the name into
	   THIS class's declarations. A nested class's implicit-receiver send reaches
	   what its enclosing class declares, plus its own superclasses - not what the
	   enclosing class inherits. `global` is declared in JSForPrimordialSoup's Js,
	   which we inherit, so without this override SessionStorage sending `global`
	   answers doesNotUnderstand. Public because the inherited member is: ordinary
	   sends of `js global` are everywhere. *)
			
			^super global
		)
		public localStorage ^<SessionStorage> = (
			(* The store this instance sees in place of the browser's localStorage. It
	   presents the same getItem:/setItem:to:/clear protocol, so code written
	   for the ordinary platform runs here unchanged, and it starts empty -
	   which is what makes every client of a session begin from the same state.

	   It used to be a view onto the browser's real localStorage, namespaced by
	   session and cleared on first touch. Two things were wrong with that. The
	   namespace came from `super localStorage at: #name`, and nothing writes a
	   localStorage item called 'name', so EVERY Croquet page on the origin
	   shared one bucket and each new page's clear wiped the others'. And even
	   keyed correctly, instances of one session running in one browser would
	   share a bucket and clear each other. Since the store is wiped at startup
	   regardless, being in the browser's storage bought nothing to weigh
	   against that.

	   Created on first use and cached, so the store lives as long as this Js
	   instance does - the life of the page. *)
			
			sessionLocalStorage isNil ifTrue: [ sessionLocalStorage:: SessionStorage new ].
			^sessionLocalStorage
		)
		public sessionId ^<String | UndefinedObject> = (
			(* The session this page joined - and the thing the per-session store must
	   be keyed by. Resolved exactly as the boot script resolves it
	   (croquet-post.js getURIParam): the URL's sessionId parameter when there
	   is one, otherwise the value that function persisted under that same key.

	   This used to read `super localStorage at: #name`, and NOTHING writes a
	   localStorage item called 'name': the `name` in croquet-post.js is a local
	   const holding getURIParam("sessionId")'s result, not a storage key. So it
	   answered nil, and every Croquet page on the origin - any session, any app
	   - shared ONE bucket, 'app-session:undefined'. Since #localStorage clears
	   the bucket on first touch each startup, opening any Croquet page wiped the
	   store that every other open Croquet page was using, and a client could
	   read another session's leftovers.

	   Answers nil only when there is no session to speak of, which under Croquet
	   means the join had no name either. *)
			
			| params raw |
			params:: [
				((global at: #URL) new: ((global at: #window) at: #location)) at: #searchParams
			] on: Exception do: [:e | nil ].
			raw:: params isNil ifTrue: [ nil ]
				ifFalse: [ [ params get: #sessionId ] on: Exception do: [:e | nil ] ].
			(raw isNil or: [ raw isKindOfString and: [ raw isEmpty ] ]) ifTrue: [
					raw:: [ super localStorage at: #sessionId ] on: Exception do: [:e | nil ]
				].
			^raw
		)
	) : ()
) : ()
